        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 756,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 721,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the digamma function - the slope of the log of the gamma function - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt turns up whenever you fit or differentiate a gamma, beta or Dirichlet distribution (the average log of a gamma-distributed value is digamma of its shape), and digamma(n + 1) + 0.5772 is the harmonic sum 1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFor positive x it rises steadily, like a logarithm: very negative near 0, crossing zero at about 1.4616 (where log gamma is lowest), -0.5772 at 1, about 1.7 at 6. Each step of 1 adds 1/x: digamma(x + 1) = digamma(x) + 1/x.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.digamma\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n0 gives minus infinity. Negative whole numbers give NaN (not a number), and between them the curve swoops from plus to minus infinity, so keep the input positive unless you mean it.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0.25 to 6 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is digamma of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0.25 to 6, drawn from -5 (bottom) to 2 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (keep start above 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.gammaln is the function this is the slope of. t.special.polygamma with n = 0 is the same thing, and higher n gives its further derivatives.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 705,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the digamma function - the slope of the log of the gamma function - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt turns up whenever you fit or differentiate a gamma, beta or Dirichlet distribution (the average log of a gamma-distributed value is digamma of its shape), and digamma(n + 1) + 0.5772 is the harmonic sum 1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFor positive x it rises steadily, like a logarithm: very negative near 0, crossing zero at about 1.4616 (where log gamma is lowest), -0.5772 at 1, about 1.7 at 6. Each step of 1 adds 1/x: digamma(x + 1) = digamma(x) + 1/x.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.digamma\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n0 gives minus infinity. Negative whole numbers give NaN (not a number), and between them the curve swoops from plus to minus infinity, so keep the input positive unless you mean it.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0.25 to 6 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is digamma of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0.25 to 6, drawn from -5 (bottom) to 2 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (keep start above 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.gammaln is the function this is the slope of. t.special.polygamma with n = 0 is the same thing, and higher n gives its further derivatives."
        },
